{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB04572 - MONDO_3792",
  "verbalization": "Thiotepa, a chemotherapy agent, is implicated in the treatment of ovarian carcinomas through multiple indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Thiotepa may exert its antitumor effects via the induction of malignant germ cell tumors in the ovary, which in turn may lead to the progression of ovarian carcinosarcomas. This is supported by the indication of Thiotepa in both immature ovarian teratomas and malignant dysgerminomatous germ cell tumors, which are histologically distinct but share a common origin in germ cell malignancies. These tumors are known to be associated with the development of ovarian carcinosarcomas, as they can serve as a precursor or a site of metastasis. The presence of a direct indication from Thiotepa to ovarian carcinosarcomas, though indirect, suggests a potential mechanistic link through the progression of germ cell tumors. The integrated evidence highlights the role of germ cell malignancies in the pathogenesis of ovarian carcinosarcomas, with Thiotepa potentially contributing to this process through its effects on these precursor lesions.",
